(8,25,2007,'Alex Rodriguez becomes the youngest player in major league history to hit 500 home runs, doing so at age 32 years and eight days — faster than anyone who came before him.','record','Alex Rodriguez','New York Yankees'),
(8,26,1939,'The first major league game is broadcast on television, as NBC''s experimental station W2XBS shows a Dodgers-Reds doubleheader to the few hundred people in New York City who own television sets.','milestone',NULL,NULL),
(8,6,1945,'Warren Spahn makes his major league debut after serving in World War II — where he earned a Purple Heart and a Bronze Star at the Battle of the Bulge — and goes on to win 363 games.','debut','Warren Spahn',NULL),
(8,22,1986,'Roger Clemens strikes out his 17th batter of the game to set a new American League record, continuing a season in which he will go 24-4 and win the Cy Young Award.','record','Roger Clemens','Boston Red Sox'),
(8,31,1935,'Babe Ruth is released by the Boston Braves, ending his major league career after 714 home runs — a number so impossibly large it will stand as the all-time record for nearly four decades.','milestone','Babe Ruth','Boston Braves'),
(8,2,1979,'Thurman Munson, the Yankees'' captain and catcher, dies in a plane crash while practicing takeoffs and landings at his home airport in Canton, Ohio — a loss that devastates a franchise and a city.','death','Thurman Munson','New York Yankees'),
(8,23,1936,'A 17-year-old Bob Feller strikes out 15 Philadelphia Athletics in only his second major league start, immediately announcing himself as one of the most electric pitching talents the game has ever seen.','debut','Bob Feller','Cleveland Indians'),
(8,19,1951,'In one of baseball''s greatest publicity stunts, 3-foot-7 Eddie Gaedel steps in to pinch-hit for the Browns, walks on four pitches, and is never seen in a major league game again — Commissioner Frick invalidates his contract the next day.','milestone','Eddie Gaedel','St. Louis Browns'),

-- ── SEPTEMBER ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(9,6,1995,'Cal Ripken Jr. plays in his 2,131st consecutive game, breaking Lou Gehrig''s "unbreakable" record before a standing ovation that lasts over 22 minutes — taking a lap around Camden Yards in one of baseball''s most emotional moments.','record','Cal Ripken Jr.','Baltimore Orioles'),
(9,23,1927,'Babe Ruth hits his 60th home run of the season off Tom Zachary of the Washington Senators, setting a record that will stand for 34 years and define an era of baseball''s greatest slugger.','record','Babe Ruth','New York Yankees'),
(9,9,1965,'Sandy Koufax retires 27 consecutive Cubs batters for a perfect game — his fourth no-hitter in four consecutive seasons — on the night that Bob Hendley pitches a one-hitter and still loses 1-0.','no_hitter','Sandy Koufax','Los Angeles Dodgers'),
(9,28,1941,'Ted Williams goes 6-for-8 in a season-ending doubleheader at Philadelphia, finishing with a .406 batting average — refusing to sit out the games and protect the .400 mark, saying "If I can''t hit .400 all the way, I don''t deserve it."','record','Ted Williams','Boston Red Sox'),
(9,29,1954,'Willie Mays makes "The Catch" — sprinting toward the center field wall at the Polo Grounds, making an over-the-shoulder grab of Vic Wertz''s 460-foot drive, spinning and throwing in one motion — a play many still call the greatest in baseball history.','record','Willie Mays','New York Giants'),
(9,1,1971,'The Pittsburgh Pirates field the first all-Black starting lineup in major league history, celebrating the legacy of integration while Roberto Clemente homers and drives in two runs.','milestone','Roberto Clemente','Pittsburgh Pirates'),
(9,8,1998,'Mark McGwire hits his 62nd home run off Steve Trachsel of the Cubs, breaking Roger Maris''s 37-year-old record in a moment of unbridled national celebration — the first ball landing barely 341 feet from home plate.','record','Mark McGwire','St. Louis Cardinals'),
(9,22,1993,'Nolan Ryan, at age 46, walks off the mound for the last time after tearing his elbow ligament — leaving the game with 5,714 career strikeouts, seven no-hitters, and a legacy no pitcher will ever approach.','milestone','Nolan Ryan','Texas Rangers'),
(9,11,1985,'Pete Rose singles off Eric Show of the Padres to record the 4,192nd hit of his career, surpassing Ty Cobb''s all-time hit record — a moment stopped by a 7-minute standing ovation and Rose weeping at first base.','record','Pete Rose','Cincinnati Reds'),
(9,18,1996,'Roger Clemens strikes out 20 batters for the second time in his career, matching his own record from 1986 — a feat no other pitcher in major league history has accomplished even once.','record','Roger Clemens','Boston Red Sox'),
(9,26,2004,'Barry Bonds walks for the 232nd time in a single season, shattering his own record of 198 set two years prior — a statistical monument to how completely pitchers refused to face him.','record','Barry Bonds','San Francisco Giants'),
(9,5,1995,'Cal Ripken Jr.''s consecutive game streak reaches 2,130 games — tying Lou Gehrig the day before he will break it — as the baseball world counts down to a moment it knows will be historic.','record','Cal Ripken Jr.','Baltimore Orioles'),
(9,26,1908,'The Giants'' Merkle''s Boner costs New York the pennant after the young first baseman fails to touch second base on a walk-off hit, leading to a forfeited out and eventually a one-game playoff the Cubs win.','milestone',NULL,'New York Giants'),
(9,30,2001,'Barry Bonds hits his 71st and 72nd home runs of the season in a doubleheader against the Dodgers, two of the most anticipated back-to-back swings in baseball history.','record','Barry Bonds','San Francisco Giants'),
(9,14,1983,'Steve Carlton becomes only the second pitcher in history to record 3,500 career strikeouts, joining Nolan Ryan in the most exclusive strikeout club in the game.','record','Steve Carlton','Philadelphia Phillies'),
(9,24,1957,'Ted Williams, in his final at-bat at Fenway Park in what he announces as his last season — he would actually come back — tips his cap to the fans for the first and only time in his legendary career.','milestone','Ted Williams','Boston Red Sox'),
(9,27,1998,'Mark McGwire finishes the season with 70 home runs, his final blast coming in the last at-bat of the year — the single-season record that would stand until Barry Bonds hits 73 in 2001.','record','Mark McGwire','St. Louis Cardinals'),
(9,1,1951,'The New York Giants begin the greatest comeback in baseball history, trailing the Dodgers by 13½ games with 44 to play — a deficit they will erase in one of the most improbable pennant races ever.','milestone',NULL,'New York Giants'),
(9,3,1950,'Vern Bickford of the Braves no-hits the Brooklyn Dodgers at Braves Field, one of 15 no-hitters thrown in a remarkable decade for the feat.','no_hitter','Vern Bickford','Boston Braves'),
(9,17,1947,'Jackie Robinson is named the first-ever MLB Rookie of the Year — the inaugural year the award exists — for a season in which he batted .297 and led the Dodgers to the World Series while enduring constant harassment and abuse.','milestone','Jackie Robinson','Brooklyn Dodgers'),
(9,28,2008,'The New York Mets lose on the final day of the season at Shea Stadium, missing the playoffs — and in the process sending off the beloved park not with a parade, but with the quiet devastation of another September collapse.','milestone',NULL,'New York Mets'),
(9,20,2003,'Sammy Sosa hits the 574th home run of his career, separating himself from Rafael Palmeiro for the most home runs ever by a player from the Dominican Republic.','record','Sammy Sosa','Chicago Cubs'),

-- ── OCTOBER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(10,8,1956,'Don Larsen of the New York Yankees retires all 27 Brooklyn Dodgers batters to throw the only perfect game in World Series history — a 2-0 win in Game 5 — achieving perfection on the sport''s biggest stage in one of baseball''s most improbable single-game performances.','world_series','Don Larsen','New York Yankees'),
(10,1,1961,'Roger Maris hits his 61st home run of the season off Tracy Stallard of the Red Sox on the final day of the year — breaking Babe Ruth''s single-season record with the weight of all of New York and all of baseball on his shoulders.','record','Roger Maris','New York Yankees'),
(10,15,1988,'Kirk Gibson, limping badly from leg injuries and appearing in only one of the World Series'' 12 plate appearances, pinch-hits in the ninth inning and drives Dennis Eckersley''s 3-2 backdoor slider over the right-field wall for one of the most dramatic home runs in baseball history.','world_series','Kirk Gibson','Los Angeles Dodgers'),
(10,13,1960,'Bill Mazeroski of the Pittsburgh Pirates hits a walk-off home run in the ninth inning of Game 7 to defeat the Yankees — still the only time in baseball history a World Series has ended on a walk-off home run by a non-DH batter.','world_series','Bill Mazeroski','Pittsburgh Pirates'),
(10,21,1975,'Carlton Fisk waves his walk-off home run fair down the left-field line to win Game 6 of the World Series for the Red Sox in the 12th inning — the image of Fisk jumping and waving the ball fair becomes one of baseball''s defining photographs.','world_series','Carlton Fisk','Boston Red Sox'),
(10,25,1986,'Mookie Wilson''s slow grounder trickles through Bill Buckner''s legs at first base with two outs in the 10th inning, giving the Mets the win in Game 6 — turning a moment into a myth and crushing Boston''s latest World Series dream.','world_series','Mookie Wilson','New York Mets'),
(10,23,1993,'Joe Carter hits a walk-off three-run homer in the bottom of the ninth of Game 6, giving the Toronto Blue Jays back-to-back World Series titles — just the second walk-off World Series-ending homer ever, after Mazeroski.','world_series','Joe Carter','Toronto Blue Jays'),
(10,27,2004,'The Boston Red Sox complete a four-game sweep of the St. Louis Cardinals to win the World Series for the first time since 1918 — ending the "Curse of the Bambino" and sending Red Sox fans who have waited their entire lives into euphoria.','world_series',NULL,'Boston Red Sox'),
(10,17,1989,'A 6.9-magnitude earthquake strikes the San Francisco Bay Area minutes before Game 3 of the World Series at Candlestick Park, postponing the Series for ten days and putting a single baseball game in its proper perspective.','world_series',NULL,NULL),
(10,4,1955,'The Brooklyn Dodgers win their first and only World Series championship — "Next Year" finally arriving — as Johnny Podres shuts out the Yankees 2-0 in Game 7, sending all of Brooklyn into the streets.','world_series',NULL,'Brooklyn Dodgers'),
(10,14,1976,'Chris Chambliss hits a walk-off home run in the bottom of the ninth off Kansas City''s Mark Littell to win the ALCS for the Yankees — with fans mobbing the field so thoroughly that Chambliss has to physically fight his way around the bases.','postseason','Chris Chambliss','New York Yankees'),
(10,2,1978,'The Yankees and Red Sox play a one-game playoff for the AL East title, with Bucky Dent hitting a three-run homer over the Green Monster — a blow that earns him a nickname in Boston that cannot be printed here.','postseason','Bucky Dent','New York Yankees'),
(10,16,2003,'Aaron Boone hits a walk-off home run in the bottom of the 11th to win the ALCS for the Yankees over the Red Sox — one game after Grady Little''s fateful decision to leave Pedro Martinez in too long.','postseason','Aaron Boone','New York Yankees'),
(10,14,1969,'The Miracle Mets defeat the Baltimore Orioles to win the World Series just seven years after going 40-120 in their first season — the most improbable championship in baseball history.','world_series',NULL,'New York Mets'),
(10,1,2022,'The Cardinals'' Albert Pujols hits his 700th career home run, joining Babe Ruth, Hank Aaron, and Barry Bonds as the only players ever to reach the milestone.','record','Albert Pujols','St. Louis Cardinals'),
(10,3,1951,'Bobby Thomson hits the "Shot Heard ''Round the World" — a walk-off three-run homer off Ralph Branca in the bottom of the ninth of the deciding playoff game — completing the Giants'' greatest comeback and destroying the Dodgers'' pennant.','postseason','Bobby Thomson','New York Giants'),
(10,18,2004,'David Ortiz hits a two-run walk-off homer in the 12th inning of Game 4 as the Red Sox begin their historic comeback from three games down — winning the next three to become the first team in baseball history to come back from 0-3.','postseason','David Ortiz','Boston Red Sox'),
(10,26,1947,'The Dodgers'' Al Gionfriddo makes a miraculous catch of DiMaggio''s drive to left-center to preserve a 6-5 win in Game 6 of the World Series, prompting the stoic DiMaggio to kick the dirt in his only visible on-field emotional display.','world_series','Al Gionfriddo','Brooklyn Dodgers'),
(10,5,2001,'Barry Bonds hits his 73rd home run of the season in the final game of the year, shattering Mark McGwire''s record of 70 from just three years before — a mark still shadowed by the era in which it was set.','record','Barry Bonds','San Francisco Giants'),
(10,24,1992,'The Toronto Blue Jays become the first non-U.S. team to win the World Series, defeating the Atlanta Braves — with Dave Winfield hitting the decisive double in Game 6 at age 41.','world_series',NULL,'Toronto Blue Jays'),
(10,15,2003,'Steve Bartman reaches for a foul pop fly in the 8th inning of Game 6 of the NLCS with the Cubs five outs from the World Series — what follows is a complete meltdown as eight runs score and the Cubs'' dreams die again.','postseason',NULL,'Chicago Cubs'),
(10,10,1920,'Elmer Smith hits the first grand slam in World Series history, and Bill Wambsganss makes an unassisted triple play — also a World Series first — on the same afternoon in Cleveland, baseball history happening so fast the crowd can barely process it.','world_series',NULL,'Cleveland Indians'),
(10,22,1975,'The Reds win Game 7 of the World Series to deny the Red Sox, completing one of the greatest October series in history and making Carlton Fisk''s heroics the night before feel even more bittersweet.','world_series',NULL,'Cincinnati Reds'),
(10,9,1916,'Babe Ruth pitches 14 consecutive scoreless innings in the World Series for the Boston Red Sox — a record that stands until Whitey Ford breaks it in 1961 — showing the world the pitcher''s talent that would soon be abandoned for a bat.','world_series','Babe Ruth','Boston Red Sox'),
(10,27,2016,'The Chicago Cubs win the World Series for the first time in 108 years, defeating the Cleveland Indians in Game 7 in an extra-inning classic — ending the longest championship drought in North American professional sports.','world_series',NULL,'Chicago Cubs'),
(10,4,1974,'Oakland wins its third consecutive World Series title, with Catfish Hunter, Rollie Fingers, and the greenand-gold machine cementing their dynasty.','world_series',NULL,'Oakland Athletics'),
(10,11,1972,'The Oakland A''s begin a dynasty run, winning the first of three consecutive World Series titles — with the "Mustache Gang" becoming baseball''s most colorful and dominant team of the era.','world_series',NULL,'Oakland Athletics'),
(10,20,2004,'David Ortiz''s walk-off single in the 14th inning of Game 5 keeps the Red Sox alive down 3-1 in the ALCS — the comeback from the brink that allows one of baseball''s great reversals to unfold.','postseason','David Ortiz','Boston Red Sox'),
(10,29,2019,'The Washington Nationals win their first World Series title, completing a remarkable comeback from 19-31 in late May to become champions — with Stephen Strasburg winning MVP after an extraordinary run through October.','world_series',NULL,'Washington Nationals'),

-- ── NOVEMBER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(11,4,2001,'Luis Gonzalez''s walk-off single off Mariano Rivera in the bottom of the ninth of Game 7 gives the Arizona Diamondbacks the World Series title over the Yankees — ending New York''s bid for a fifth consecutive championship.','world_series','Luis Gonzalez','Arizona Diamondbacks'),
(11,2,1999,'The New York Yankees complete their sweep of the Atlanta Braves to win their second World Series in three years, with Roger Clemens, Orlando Hernandez, and Mariano Rivera dominating one of the great pitching staffs of the era.','world_series',NULL,'New York Yankees'),
(11,7,2000,'The New York Yankees win their fourth World Series in five years, defeating the Mets in the "Subway Series" — Derek Jeter''s leadoff homer in Game 4 the defining moment of a dynasty''s last great October.','world_series','Derek Jeter','New York Yankees'),
(11,2,2016,'Kris Bryant fields a ground ball, throws to Anthony Rizzo, and the Chicago Cubs are World Series champions for the first time in 108 years — five hours and ten innings of pure baseball drama.','world_series','Kris Bryant','Chicago Cubs'),
(11,1,2023,'The Texas Rangers win their first World Series title by defeating the Arizona Diamondbacks in Game 5 on the road — completing their improbable run from no postseason berth in 2022 to champions.','world_series',NULL,'Texas Rangers'),
(11,4,2015,'The Kansas City Royals defeat the Mets to win the World Series — Eric Hosmer scoring from third on a Salvador Perez single — bringing the franchise its first championship since 1985.','world_series',NULL,'Kansas City Royals'),
(11,3,2010,'The San Francisco Giants win their first World Series title since 1954, with Edgar Renteria''s three-run homer in Game 5 and Madison Bumgarner''s complete-game dominance silencing the Rangers.','world_series',NULL,'San Francisco Giants'),
(11,8,2022,'The Hall of Fame announces the rules for a special Contemporary Era Committee, establishing a pathway for players overlooked by the BBWAA to be considered by a committee of baseball experts.','hall_of_fame',NULL,NULL),
(11,5,2014,'The San Francisco Giants win an extraordinary Game 7 against the Kansas City Royals, with Madison Bumgarner pitching five innings of one-hit relief on two days'' rest in the most dominant World Series pitching performance in the modern era.','world_series','Madison Bumgarner','San Francisco Giants'),
(11,12,2009,'Joe DiMaggio''s 1941 World Series rings are sold at auction for $290,000 each, reminding a new generation of fans that the Yankee Clipper''s career was measured not just in statistics but in championships.','milestone','Joe DiMaggio',NULL),

-- ── DECEMBER ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(12,31,1971,'Roberto Clemente''s plane crashes into the Atlantic Ocean shortly after takeoff from San Juan, Puerto Rico, killing all aboard. The Pirates outfielder was delivering earthquake relief supplies to Nicaragua.','death','Roberto Clemente','Pittsburgh Pirates'),
(12,8,1976,'The free-agent auction era begins in earnest as Reggie Jackson signs with the Yankees for five years and $2.96 million — and several other marquee players sign seven-figure deals on the same day.','milestone','Reggie Jackson','New York Yankees'),
(12,13,1975,'Arbitrator Peter Seitz rules in the Messersmith-McNally case, declaring that players are not bound to their teams in perpetuity — a decision that effectively creates free agency and permanently changes the economics of baseball.','milestone',NULL,NULL),
(12,3,1958,'Roy Campanella, the Dodgers'' three-time MVP catcher, is left paralyzed by a car accident on an icy road in New York. The following May, 93,000 fans attend a tribute game at the Los Angeles Coliseum.','death','Roy Campanella','Los Angeles Dodgers'),
(12,12,2007,'The Mitchell Report is released, naming 89 major league players connected to performance-enhancing drugs — a landmark day for the sport''s reckoning with the Steroid Era.','milestone',NULL,NULL),
(12,9,1966,'Sandy Koufax announces his retirement at age 30, walking away from the game at the peak of his powers rather than risk permanent damage to his arthritic pitching elbow.','milestone','Sandy Koufax','Los Angeles Dodgers'),
(12,1,1974,'Oakland''s Catfish Hunter is declared a free agent after arbitrator Peter Seitz rules that Charlie Finley failed to make an insurance payment — the first modern free agent, with 23 teams bidding on his services.','milestone','Catfish Hunter','Oakland Athletics'),
(12,9,2011,'Albert Pujols signs a 10-year, $240 million contract with the Los Angeles Angels — the third-largest contract in baseball history — leaving the Cardinals after 11 seasons and three World Series titles.','milestone','Albert Pujols','Los Angeles Angels'),
(12,7,1993,'The Florida Marlins, Tampa Bay Devil Rays, Arizona Diamondbacks, and Colorado Rockies receive expansion approval — the last major expansion in MLB history to this point, bringing the league to 30 teams.','milestone',NULL,NULL),
(12,5,2006,'Daisuke Matsuzaka is posted to the Boston Red Sox, who pay $51 million just for the right to negotiate — beginning a Japanese import phenomenon that brings some of the world''s best pitchers to the American market.','milestone','Daisuke Matsuzaka','Boston Red Sox'),
(12,25,1996,'A Christmas Day snowstorm in Denver buries Coors Field in 14 inches of snow — a reminder that Colorado''s home opener will always be one of the most reliably weathered events in baseball.','milestone',NULL,'Colorado Rockies'),
(12,16,2009,'The Yankees sign Mark Teixeira to an eight-year deal in December, capping one of the most expensive off-season spending sprees in baseball history — and winning the World Series the following October.','milestone','Mark Teixeira','New York Yankees'),
(12,11,1974,'The Oakland A''s dynasty fractures when the three-time World Series champions begin losing their core players — the first sign that Finley''s refusal to pay market rates will dismantle the greatest baseball team of the decade.','milestone',NULL,'Oakland Athletics'),
(12,4,2020,'The Baseball Writers'' Association announces the finalists for the 2021 Hall of Fame ballot — including Curt Schilling, Barry Bonds, and Roger Clemens — setting up a contentious vote in the Steroid Era''s most complicated year.','hall_of_fame',NULL,NULL);
